## Change log

- `[admin+app] 2026-09-04` — **The database was being taken down by phones, not by the panel, and the fix has to work on installs that will never update.** Last 24h on production: ~9M requests at 25% success. Four paths made almost all of it — `GET app_settings` (~2.7M: the app's `live_db_refresh` polled every **5s** and each failed tick cascaded through up to five column-fallback selects), `rpc/driver_report_location` (~2.1M: builds before `1.1.16+76` still fall back to a **2s + `force`** durable write whenever the edge rail fails, and `force` bypasses coalesce), `GET drivers` (~1.25M, the same 5s coordinator) and `GET /auth/v1/user` (~1.25M: the Worker validated every ingest JWT, expired duty tokens 403'd every time, and the requests carried no `User-Agent`). The compute upgrade to 2XL buys headroom; it does not change any of those numbers.

 **Chicken-and-egg, stated plainly:** installed APKs have no force-update UI, so a force-update toggle cannot reach them. The only levers that work on an old install are server-side, so those shipped first and need no Play release. `driver_report_location` loads the previous row first and, unless `p_tracking_status = 'delivery_submit'`, returns `{ coalesced: true }` when the new fix is within `app_settings.driver_location_rpc_min_interval_seconds` (default 15) and under 18 m of the last one — **even when `p_force_history` is true**. `delivery_submit` is exempt because that is the fix taken *at* submission and must be written. The Worker caches **negative** JWT results (a definitively rejected token is not re-asked for 5 minutes) and sets `User-Agent: dpd-live/fleet-room`; token resolution became tri-state (`ok` / `rejected` / `unavailable`) so a GoTrue 5xx during an outage is *not* cached as a rejection — that would have signed the fleet out of the edge rail for five minutes every time auth blipped. `token-resolution.test.ts` pins the split.

 **Force update is an admin-controlled minimum `versionCode`, not a Play API lookup.** `app_settings` gains `driver_app_force_update` / `driver_app_min_version_code` / `driver_app_min_version_name` / `driver_app_update_message`, editable on `/settings/app` next to maintenance (`logAdminActivity` on save). "Latest on Play" is the build number the operator just published — they know it from `pubspec.yaml` and the Play Console, and a Play Developer API integration would have been a credential and a failure mode for a number that changes a few times a month. The four columns ride the existing anon `app_settings_public_branding_read` row policy so the gate works before login. `driver-passcode-login` reads them **before** validating credentials and returns **426 `update_required`** — no session is minted for a blocked build, and no passcode is validated for it either.

 **App side (Play `1.1.20+83`).** `AppBranding` carries the four fields; the router redirect sends every route to a non-dismissible `/update-required` when `forceUpdateBlocks()` says so, and that gate outranks maintenance, because a blocked build must not be able to reach a screen that keeps polling. A login refused with 426 raises a process-lifetime `ForceUpdateDemand` the router also honours, since the edge function read the authoritative row and the phone's copy may be stale or from the offline cache. The screen offers Play Store (`market://` then https) and Check again, and nothing else. An install that cannot report its `versionCode` is treated as below any minimum, mirroring the server — both sides must agree or a rider passes the router and is refused at login with no screen that explains why. `live_db_refresh` polls **60s** instead of 5s (Realtime `UPDATE` on `app_settings` is the primary signal and is unchanged), the branding fetch is **one select per refresh** (the column-fallback cascade now steps down only on a genuine `42703 undefined_column` and remembers the step for the process, instead of running five selects on every network blip), and the login-verification gate stops re-reading `app_settings` for a flag the branding provider already caches. `force_update_gate_test.dart` covers the rule, the 426 parser and the demand notifier.

 **Rollout order matters and the toggle stays OFF.** Server kill-switches are live now. The admin UI is live now. The gated build has to reach Play and be installed before the toggle does anything useful — turning it on today would refuse login to every phone in the fleet with no screen telling the rider why. Once `1.1.20+83` is live on Play: set the minimum to `83`, switch **Force app update** on, and every older install is trapped on Update Required the next time it reads settings or tries to log in. Migrations `20261013100000`, `20261013100100`; `driver-passcode-login` v3; Worker deployed. Ops notes: the MCP `apply_migration` path stamps its own timestamp versions, so the ledger was repaired by hand again (delete strays, insert filename versions); `wrangler` 4 needs Node 22 and this machine runs 20 — `npx -y -p node@22 -c 'node ./node_modules/wrangler/bin/wrangler.js deploy'` works without touching the global Node.

- `[admin only] 2026-09-03` — **Page-level auth and branding now abort the same way the proxy does, so a hung GoTrue call cannot hold the dashboard after the 504 gate is gone.** The screenshot some operators saw was `MIDDLEWARE_INVOCATION_TIMEOUT` from Mumbai (`bom1`) — the proxy fix already shipped, but `createClient()` used by `requireAuth` and the locale layout still had no `AbortSignal`. A 522 that the proxy now skips would have become an 8–15s blank paint, or worse a `maybeSingle()`/`single()` miss read as signed-out. Timeouts are opt-in on those two clients only (8s session, 5s settings/branding); report RPCs stay uncapped. A failed profile read is `unavailable`, not a login redirect.

- `[admin only] 2026-09-03` — **The panel 504'd on every route because the proxy had no timeout on anything, and a degraded read signed admins out.** The error page named it exactly: `MIDDLEWARE_INVOCATION_TIMEOUT`. Vercel terminates Routing Middleware at **25s**, and that budget covers every call `proxy.ts` makes — `getUser()`, its retry, then `app_settings` and `profiles`. **None of them had a timeout.** Supabase failures here are slow rather than fast (the probe's own comment records that a Cloudflare 522 in front of GoTrue surfaces after ~15s), so one hung auth call plus the two reads exceeds the budget and the matcher covers every path, which is why the whole panel went down at once rather than one page.

  **The cap has to abort, not just race.** `createTimeoutFetch` is passed to `createServerClient` as `global.fetch` and applies `AbortSignal.timeout`, which is Vercel's own remedy for this error and releases the socket instead of leaving a pending request behind; `withDeadline` remains as a backstop for work that never reached the network. An abort reaches auth-js as `AuthRetryableFetchError`, which `isInfrastructureFailure` already classifies, so a timeout lands on `unavailable: true` and the existing "unproven, not absent" branch carries it — the proxy returns the response untouched and the page runs its own gate. 3s for the probe and 2.5s for the reads are ~20x a healthy round trip, so they never fire in normal operation. The timeout also lands *after* `RETRY_IF_FAILED_WITHIN_MS`, so a slow failure still does not buy a second wait.

  **The second defect was next to it and is the logout users reported.** `maybeSingle()` reports a missing row and a failed read identically as `data: null`, and the protected branch reads `!profileRow?.admin_role_id` as grounds for `/login?error=not_authorized` — so a transient blip bounced a valid admin to the login screen. That is the same hazard the file already reasons about for the probe ("signing the admin out here is the one outcome that is certainly wrong"), simply never applied to the profile. `guardedRead` separates the two, and a failed read now skips every profile-driven redirect. `Promise.all` over the raw builders was also unguarded, so a rejection at the fetch layer threw out of the middleware entirely; it cannot now.

  **And the page gate would have undone the proxy fix on its own.** `requireAuth` called `getSessionUser()`, which drops the `unavailable` flag, so an unreachable auth backend produced `session === null` and a redirect to `/login` — the proxy correctly falling through only to have the page log the admin out a moment later. It now reads `getSessionOutcome()` and **throws** when the session is unproven, which hands the request to `[locale]/error.tsx`; that boundary already existed for this ("a failed auth round trip used to redirect to /login and read as a logout") and nothing had ever routed to it. A genuinely absent session still redirects, because that one is a fact. Three sites, one bug: absent and unverifiable are not the same state, and every layer that collapsed them signed someone out.

  Every remaining branch was already fail-open and stays that way — `super_admin_claimed` defaults to `true` and `maintenance_mode` to falsy, so a failed ops read redirects nowhere. **`app_settings` is cached per isolate for 60s**, removing a round trip per navigation for a row that changes perhaps twice in the project's life; maintenance mode is safe stale there because `(dashboard)/layout.tsx` re-reads it uncached on every render, making the proxy redirect an optimisation rather than the enforcement point. An **unclaimed** super admin is deliberately never cached — that is the one window where the flag actually flips, and staleness would bounce the new super admin back to a claim page they had just finished. No migration, no schema change. `npm run test:auth` covers 11 cases, including that a fallback timer cannot outlive the response.

- `[admin only] 2026-09-03` — **Orders Report now takes from/to time, and the sheet only counts deliveries in that Kuwait window.** `/deliveries` Generate used calendar dates and then scanned two extra days so overnight shifts on the last date were not lost — which also meant a 08:00–18:00 ask still counted the rest of those days. The dialog has `h-9` time inputs (default 00:00–23:59), seeds from the list date filter when one is set, and `report_delivery_orders` clips `delivered_at` to the chosen clock (to-minute inclusive). Shift-day columns are unchanged. Migration `20261012100000`.

- `[admin+app] 2026-09-03` — **Live GPS stays on Cloudflare; a dead edge no longer writes every fix into Postgres.** Idle `batchSize: 2` was POSTing parked phones every ~2s and burning the Worker quota; batching is moving-only and idle waits 30s. A failed `/ingest` used to call `driver_report_location(..., force)` on the same 2s pass, which bypasses coalesce and is what flooded the project login shares. Fallback is now 15s moving / 30s idle without `force`. FleetRoom alarms back off from 2s to 30s when no admin socket is open and ingest has been quiet for 3 minutes. Workers Paid is required so ingest does not die on free-plan 1027. App login ships alphanumeric employee ID on Play `1.1.16+76`. No migration.

- `[admin+app] 2026-09-02` — **Nine RCM QA reports: hide Request documents, ack file links, audit Read after Update.** Sick-leave Pending HR Review still offered Request documents after the rider's medical file was already on `request_attachments` — the action is hidden once any attachment exists. Ack "Upload documents" stuffed storage keys into `payload.driver_ack_note`, so Admin painted a raw path under Rider Note; `driver_acknowledge_request` now takes `p_attachment_keys` (same insert as clarify), the app sends keys separately, and Admin parses legacy `Documents uploaded:` notes into clickable links. The app Admin response card no longer reprints a clarification question as Comment from admin. Upload Document footer buttons share height / single-line labels; the source sheet is titled Choose Source; fuel Evidence chips ellipsis instead of wrapping `.png` onto the next line. E-Signature Settings opened from the hub drops the stray Settings crumb. Request-detail Read is logged on page open only (keyed by `requestId`) so a decide refetch cannot sit above Update. All Requests date preset `all` is labelled All dates. Migration `20261011100000`.

- `[admin only] 2026-09-01` — **Hub request counts were real; This month hid them.** `admin_count_requests_by_type` has no date window, so a Sick/Accident tile can read `1` for `RCM-0074` filed 31 Aug 15:30 Kuwait. All Requests defaulted to `this_month` on `created_at`, and on 1 Sep that window is empty — KPI Total 0, “+ 40 vs last month”, generic empty state. The queue now defaults to All (hub tiles pass `preset=all`); This month remains a filter. Empty copy names the date mismatch when filters hide every row.

- `[admin+app] 2026-09-01` — **Eleven RCM QA reports: attach actions, clarify upload, Need By, sick-leave Other, salary response.** Attach & Send / Attach Breakdown opened no picker — they were `runAction` aliases for approve. Both now open a footer-first file dialog, upload to `request-attachments`, and `admin_decide_request` returns `attachment_required` when `p_meta.attachments` is empty. Clarification Add attachment was visible but gallery/camera errors died silently and a missing session returned no keys; the box is tappable, picks report a snackbar, and the RPC also inserts `request_attachments` rows. All Requests still has no checkbox on decided statuses (Aug 27), but the select column no longer vanishes when the current page has none left to decide. Signature Categories breadcrumbs drop the stray Settings crumb. Document Need By cannot be before Kuwait today (picker + `rcm_validate`). Send Response still requires a reason; the placeholder now names that action, and the rider Admin response card shows the note for salary / complaint `responded`. Sick leave paints From then To, names which date is missing, and shows a required “Specify leave type” field when Other is selected. New Request no longer autofocuses the native date input. Migration `20261010100000`.

- `[admin+app] 2026-08-29` — **On-behalf complaint details were dumping the payload, and clarify had no attach.** Complaint (and any type without handwritten rows) printed `salary_issues`, `created_on_behalf: true`, the staff UUID and the raw ISO time because `_typedDetailRows` returned `[]` and the leftover map was stringified. Category now uses `complaint_categories` labels, on-behalf shows the staff name and a formatted time only, and internals / UUIDs never become rows. Clarification already accepted `p_attachment_keys` for every type; Add attachment now sits under Your response on all of them, not only document / sick leave.

- `[admin+app] 2026-08-28` — **Nine RCM QA reports: leave/asset create, audit details, asset tabs, loan deduction date.** Admin New Request treated `leave_type` / `asset_type` as free text while `rcm_validate_request_input` only accepts the seeded catalogues, so on-behalf Leave and Asset failed with a raw `invalid_option:…` toast and Asset Type had no dropdown. Both fields (and the other static selects) now read `request_field_definitions.options`, with the same catalogues as a fallback, and the toast maps `invalid_option` / `field_required` / `date_in_past`. Loan Needed-by cannot be before Kuwait today (picker `min` + server). Asset status tabs drop Rescheduled / Overdue / Solved / Responded, matching fuel.

  The Requests audit list was printing All-Requests `preset: this_month` (or nothing, after UUIDs and term objects were stripped), so Today / This Week looked like This Month and many Details cells were blank. List-scan rows are omitted; remaining rows name the request and the action. Request details (admin + app) use a fixed label column with start-aligned wrapping values. The driver-app Admin response card stays after acknowledgement on approved / closed loan, asset and sick-leave, and always lists Deduction start date.

- `[admin only] 2026-08-28` — **The Period tab was empty because `max(jsonb)` does not exist.** Phase 2 joined the per-driver criteria map into the team `GROUP BY` and collapsed it with `MAX(rcm.manual_criteria)`. Postgres has no `max(jsonb)`, so `admin_list_driver_performance` raised 42883 and the page painted "No drivers in range" — the same empty state as a genuine miss, against 47 worked drivers and 3,595 deliveries in the open window. Collapse is now `ARRAY_AGG(...)[1]` (the copies are identical). The page also gained a distinct load-failed state so a dead RPC cannot look like an empty fleet again. Migration `20261005100320`.

- `[admin only] 2026-08-28` — **The analysis tab answers "is the fleet getting better", and it refuses to answer when it cannot.** `/performance` could rank today and drill into one driver, but management's actual question is a direction, and nothing on the page held more than one period.

