 **The distance, and the extra pointers, are the same bug.** Android's network provider reports `accuracy_meters` of exactly **100** from a tower that can be 600m away — and the app's coarse gate was `> 100`, so those fixes passed. The pin ping-ponged, the trail drew the hop, and the odometer added a few hundred metres every couple of seconds: 65.7 km logged today for a rider who stayed inside one block, against 7.1 km of sampled history. 50m is now the threshold in all four places (app heartbeat, room ingest, both odometer writers, day-route point selection); 50 sits above a real GPS fix in an urban canyon (~20-40m) and below every coarse fix seen in production. Both writers also refuse a segment implying >40 m/s, which is the rule the route already used. In the room a coarse fix is **deferred, not dropped** — it still reaches the durable history, it just does not move a pin whose accurate fix is under two minutes old. The app's last-good pin outranks a coarse fix for the same two minutes and no longer yields to `force`, which decides *when* to report, not which fix to trust.

 With the odometer trustworthy, the 4.6 km / 3.5 km disagreement is closed by sharing it: `admin_get_driver_day_route` returns the stored odometer as `distance_m` for today (a past day keeps the sampled sum; `sampled_distance_m` and `distance_source` are always present). Deriving the card's figure from history instead would have put a ~350k-row day scan inside an RPC called every 60s by the room and every 10s per polling client; the odometer is maintained in O(1) by code that already holds both endpoints. The remaining "multiple pointers" are now cosmetic and fixed as such: a trail whose bounding box spans **< 25m** is not drawn (a parked phone still emits a point every 3s, and ~200 of them are a coloured blob on the marker), and route stops and the playhead became hollow rings, since a filled coloured disc on this map means "a rider is here, and this is their status".

 **Offline after a re-clock-in** was the isolate cache again: the restarted service published with the token the clock-out cleared and a duty version below the one the room held, earning `409 stale_duty_state` for the rest of the shift. `DutySessionStorage.reload()` runs on `onStart` and once per watchdog tick — never on the 1Hz path — and `_dutyStateVersion` stays captured once, because re-reading it per tick would let a zombie service pick up the bump meant to retire it. Server side, a `check_in_at` different from the one the room holds is proof of a new session, so the comparison starts over. Migration `20260916100000`; tests `fleet-live-state.test.ts` (room-level, Supabase stubbed at `fetch`), `trailSpanMeters` cases, four new heartbeat cases.

