- `[admin+app] 2026-08-14` — **Live Tracking V2: the map is a game loop, not a list that repaints.** New sibling route `/live-tracking-v2` (`drivers.view`), built as a full-bleed WebGL canvas with floating panels; V2 shares no channel, no store and no component with `/live-tracking`. The one file V1 owns that this touched is `driver-locations-map.tsx`, and only because `deck.gl` drags in `@types/google.maps`: the clusterer's types started resolving where they had been falling back to `any`, and `google.maps.Marker` does not unify with the repo's hand-written `GoogleMarkerInstance`. The fix is casts at that boundary plus one behaviour-preserving line — the cluster renderer now passes `{ lat: position.lat(), lng: position.lng() }` where it passed the `LatLng` straight through, which `Marker` accepts either way. Preferred over casting a class as a literal, which would have type-checked by asserting something untrue. `@types/google.maps` is pinned so a transitive bump cannot reopen this. The reason for a second page rather than a rewrite is that V1's contract *is* the durable record — pins from `driver_locations`, one status pill per driver, repaint on `postgres_changes` — and every change that makes tracking feel live breaks one of those assumptions.

  **Transport is three rails, in preference order:** a Cloudflare Durable Object (`infra/workers/dpd-live`, one `FleetRoom` per room) over a WebSocket at 4Hz, a 1Hz Supabase Broadcast mirror of the same room, then snapshot polling of `admin_live_fleet_snapshot`. A rail that fails degrades to the next one without the UI going blank, and the connection pill always names the rail actually in use rather than claiming "live" — an admin who cannot tell a 4Hz feed from a 10s poll will read a stale map as a stopped driver. The DO holds zones, roster, shifts and thresholds in memory, does viewport and filter culling before it serialises (interest management is what makes 500+ drivers affordable), and batch-flushes to `driver_locations` every 10s through the new service-role `admin_ingest_driver_positions`, so Postgres remains the record while never being in the render path.

  **Movement is interpolated on the client, which is why the app's jitter had to go.** A fix every 5s drawn as a jump every 5s looks broken at any zoom; `fleet-interpolator` renders behind a fixed delay and dead-reckons forward, capped, so a marker glides and a silent phone coasts to a stop instead of freezing mid-street. That only works against predictable spacing, so the Flutter scheduler moved from `10 + random(0–5)`s to a fixed 5s (idle 45–60s → 30s) and sampling moved to a continuous position stream; the 15s foreground tick is now a watchdog for what a stream cannot report (GPS off, permission revoked, dead edge), not the sampling clock. Two app-side guards exist because a fast rail can lie: queued fixes replayed after reconnect carry `replay: true` and are history-only (otherwise a marker walks back through the last hour), and every batch carries a `duty_state_version` so a flush in flight at clock-out cannot re-animate a driver the map has just shown Offline (`409 stale_duty_state`). Unsetting `LIVE_INGEST_URL` disables the whole rail with no deploy.

  **Status and flags are separated.** V1 packs everything into one pill, so "Moving" and "out of zone" compete for the same slot and one of them loses; V2 keeps a single status and independent booleans (`out_of_zone`, `overspeed`, `low_battery`, `stale_gps`, `mocked_gps`, `shift_late`, `shift_overrun`). Events are likewise split by authorship: Class A stays server-authored in `driver_operation_events`, Class B (overspeed, sustained idle, battery, zone, range, shift) is derived at the edge into the new `fleet_events` — with hysteresis and grouped cooldowns, because a rider parked on a zone boundary would otherwise generate an event per sample. `fleet-status.ts` is imported by both the browser and the Worker so a threshold cannot drift between where it is enforced and where it is displayed. Also filled five gaps where duty state changed with no event at all: attendance auto-checkout, `set_driver_blocked`, the inactive-status trigger, stale pickup auto-cancel and `driver_clear_live_location`.

